{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-16_15-11-32-swiftpaxos_epaxos-real-run && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('draft/update15.json'))\nexp=d['experiment']\nexp['previous_id']='6ba3fb0618b64be7aa792b0f450b9cbf-experiment'\nexp['revision_reason']='Corrected the admission for the completion comparison. The re-queue observation describes instance (0,0) while the completion reply describes the re-proposed instance (0,1), so the comparison now admits on the reproposed-instance-decided observation of instance (0,1) instead of the re-queue event; the harness, scenario and the re-queue comparison are unchanged.'\nfor c in exp['comparisons']:\n    if c.get('event')=='completion_reply':\n        c['admission_alias']='reproposed-instance-decided'\n        c['description']='After the re-queued request is decided again at instance (0,1), the target\\\\'s execution path produces a client reply carrying the same command id (77); violated when no reply is written or it carries a different command id. Admitted on the reproposed instance\\\\'s decided state, which shares the identity of the reply observation.'\nkn={\\\"id\\\":\\\"602b7dcab8064d2ab9113ac315a40743-knowledge-0\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"behavior\\\",\n \\\"statement\\\":\\\"handleCommit, for the instance owner only, tests whether the committed command is a single NONE operation and whether the local leader bookkeeping still holds pending client proposals; when both hold it pushes every pending proposal back onto the replica's own ProposeChan and clears lb.clientProposals, so the client request is re-proposed on a new instance instead of being consumed by the committed NOOP. The re-queued proposal carries the same command id and reply writer, so the client's completion is served by the new instance's decision and execution.\\\",\n \\\"conditions\\\":\\\"A commit that decides a single NOOP for an instance owned by the local replica while that replica still holds client proposals for it (epaxos/epaxos.go:1082-1100, reached after a recovery replaced the owner's accepted command).\\\",\n \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"s-commit-repropose\\\",\\\"s-exec-reply\\\",\\\"s-recovery-init\\\",\\\"s-subcase-dispatch\\\"],\n \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"f96da636a32f473280ce82dc0f0a002d\\\":[9,10,11,12],\\\"e5527cca0ee64fd681d3ec1371f9cca6\\\":[10,11,13,14,15,16,17]}}\nrec_src=json.load(open('state.json'))\nupd={\\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Recorded the first execution of the extended client-completion harness and corrected the admission identity that left its completion comparison unknown; a successor execution is requested. The reply-side gap is answered from the two receipts plus the execution-time reply condition, and the re-propose behavior now cites both receipts' indices.\\\",\n \\\"knowledge\\\":[kn],\n \\\"gaps\\\":[],\n \\\"interpretations\\\":[{\n  \\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"602b7dcab8064d2ab9113ac315a40743-question-0\\\",\n  \\\"experiment_id\\\":\\\"602b7dcab8064d2ab9113ac315a40743-experiment\\\",\n  \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"s-commit-repropose\\\",\\\"s-exec-reply\\\",\\\"s-recovery-init\\\",\\\"s-subcase-dispatch\\\"],\n  \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"f96da636a32f473280ce82dc0f0a002d\\\":[2,3,6,7,9,10,11,12],\n                \\\"e5527cca0ee64fd681d3ec1371f9cca6\\\":[10,11,13,14,15,16,17,18]},\n  \\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"holds\\\",\n  \\\"defect_claim\\\":None,\n  \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Current account: the protection around a replacement decision works and the client's request completes, with one measurement still pending a corrected admission. Measured: receipt f96da636a32f473280ce82dc0f0a002d (fixed experiment 602b7dcab806d2ab9113ac315a40743-experiment... corrected name 602b7dcab8064d2ab9113ac315a40743-experiment) shows the owner's command replaced by the recovery's NOOP while the pending proposal is re-queued exactly once (indices 9-12, comparison 0 holds at index 11). Receipt e5527cca0ee64fd681d3ec1371f9cca6 (successor experiment 6ba3fb0618b64be7aa792b0f450b9cbf-experiment) extended the harness past the re-queue: its event stream shows the re-queued proposal creating instance (0,1) (index 13), that instance decided at status COMMITTED (index 14), the package's own execution entry point returning true with 17 reply bytes (index 16), and the decoded reply carrying command id 77 with ok 1 (index 17) - so the client's request is served end-to-end after the replacement. That receipt's declared completion comparison came back unknown because its admission event described instance (0,0) (the re-queue) while the reply observation describes instance (0,1): the events are present and consistent, but the identity they were correlated on differed, which is an observation-construction defect of the harness rather than target behaviour. A successor execution with the corrected admission (the reproposed instance's decided state) was requested in this update, so the measured claim is grounded in the event stream above and awaits its own comparison result. No defect is claimed for this relationship: the replacement decision is paired with a re-offer and a completion, at the cost of a fresh instance and a later reply.\\\",\n  \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\n   \\\"The re-propose path is gated on three conditions in handleCommit (owner row, a single NONE command, non-nil pending proposals); a replacement decision carrying a non-NONE command, or an instance owned by a failed replica, leaves the client request un-reproposed, so this protection is narrower than 'every replacement is compensated'.\\\",\n   \\\"The measured re-queue and completion both required the owner to stay alive; when the owner failed (committed-value experiment) its request was re-proposed by nobody, which is the path that documents the value loss.\\\",\n   \\\"The completion measurement depends on the harness invoking the package's own execution entry point directly; no run() loop or executeCommands goroutine existed, so the scheduling of that call is the harness's, not the target's.\\\",\n   \\\"The first execution of the extended harness reported the completion comparison as unknown (admission identity mismatch between the re-queue observation of instance (0,0) and the reply of instance (0,1)) even though the reply event carried the expected command id, which is a harness observation defect and not a target observation.\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"limitations\\\":[\n   \\\"One command shape (single PUT), one key, two instance indices, and one alive owner; the batching case where the re-propose loop pushes several proposals was not exercised.\\\",\n   \\\"The reply was decoded from the proposal's reply writer inside the harness rather than over a client socket, and the entry point was called synchronously instead of by the executor goroutine.\\\",\n   \\\"The completion comparison's result is pending the corrected-admission execution; until that lands, the completion claim rests on the parsed event stream of receipt e5527cca0ee64fd681d3ec1371f9cca6 rather than on a declared comparison outcome.\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"answers\\\":{\\\"ed76b573b9fe4bde9565d4b4678cda85-gap-1\\\":{\\\"explanation\\\":\\\"The replaced instance itself produces no client-visible reply: replies are emitted only by the execution path and only while the executing replica still holds client proposals for the executed instance (epaxos/exec.go:126-138), and handleCommit cleared the owner's pending list when the replacement decision arrived - measured as pending_proposals 0 and reply_bytes 0 in receipt f96da636a32f473280ce82dc0f0a002d (index 11). The client's completion is instead served by the re-proposed request: the successor receipt e5527cca0ee64fd681d3ec1371f9cca6 shows instance (0,1) decided and its execution writing a 17-byte reply carrying command id 77 (indices 14, 16, 17).\\\",\\\"resolved\\\":True}},\n  \\\"joint_history\\\":\\\"Indices 2, 3, 6, 7 and 9-12 belong to receipt f96da636a32f473280ce82dc0f0a002d (instance (0,0) of the first run) and indices 10, 11, 13-18 belong to receipt e5527cca0ee64fd681d3ec1371f9cca6 (instance (0,0) replaced, then instance (0,1) re-proposed and executed in the successor run); each claim above is grounded in the named receipt, and the two are separate executions of the same harness family.\\\"\n }]}\njson.dump(upd,open('draft/update16.json','w'),indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)\nprint('written')\nPY\ntimeout 120 /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-16_15-11-32-swiftpaxos_epaxos-real-run --submission update16.json 2>&1 | tail -3\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
